+##### onRetry(context)
+
+Type: `Function`
+
+Callback invoked only when another attempt is going to happen, right before the wait. Receives the same context object as `onFailedAttempt`.
+
+Unlike `onFailedAttempt`, it is not called for the final failure, so it suits counting retries for metrics.
+
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt`, `shouldConsumeRetry`, and `shouldRetry`. It is also called when `shouldConsumeRetry` returns `false`, since the next attempt still happens, straight away.
+
+The function is _not_ called when the error is not retried, on `AbortError`, or on an abort through `signal`.
+
+The function can return a promise, which is awaited before the wait starts. If the function throws, all retries will be aborted and the original promise will reject with the thrown error.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+const run = async () => { … };
+
+const result = await pRetry(run, {
+	onRetry: ({error, attemptNumber, retryDelay}) => {
+		metrics.increment('retries');
+	}
+});
+```
+
 ##### shouldRetry(context)
 
 Type: `Function`
@@ -224,6 +250,17 @@ Measured with a monotonic clock (`performance.now()`) so system clock adjustment
 
 The limit is checked after failed attempts and callbacks, before scheduling a retry. The retry delay is shortened to the remaining time, but the scheduled retry can still start after the limit. Attempts and callbacks are never interrupted, so they can run past the limit.
 
+##### initialDelay
+
+Type: `number`\
+Default: `0`
+
+The number of milliseconds to wait before the first attempt.
+
+With the default of `0` no timer is scheduled and the first attempt starts straight away. A fractional value rounds up to a whole millisecond, and a value above 2147483647ms is clamped to that, like the other delays.
+
+The wait is not counted towards `maxRetryTime`, which starts when the first attempt does. If `signal` aborts during the wait, the promise rejects with the abort reason and the operation is never called.
+
 ##### signal
 
 Type: [`AbortSignal`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal)
